// Unit 3: Options. Chapter 2: Pricing & the Greeks.
export default {
  id: 'options-ch2',
  title: 'Pricing & the Greeks',
  blurb: 'Why an option costs what it does, what implied volatility really means, and the five Greek letters that predict how a price will move.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'options-2-what-drives-price',
      title: 'What Sets an Option Price',
      summary: `Six inputs decide what an option is worth. Learn which way each one pushes calls and puts, and the big idea behind Black-Scholes without the scary math.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'calculator',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why does this call cost $2.00?', html: `<p>In Chapter 1, Leo paid $2.00 for an LMNB 55 call. Where did that number come from?</p><p>In the end, an option's price is whatever buyers and sellers agree on. But most of the quotes you see are posted by <span class="term" data-def="Firms that continuously post bids and asks, earning the spread and hedging their risk.">market makers</span>, and they lean on pricing models to decide what to bid and ask. Those models start from the same short list of inputs.</p><p>Learn those inputs and the direction each one pushes, and option prices stop looking random. You will be able to look at two quotes and explain why one costs triple the other, which is most of what separates a confused option buyer from an informed one.</p><p>Good news: you do not need calculus. You need six ideas and a feel for which way each one tilts the scale.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Six dials control every option price. Turn one, the price moves. This lesson shows you which way.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The six inputs', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>If this goes up...</th><th>Calls</th><th>Puts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stock price</td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td><td><span class="down">Down</span></td></tr><tr><td>Strike price (choosing a higher one)</td><td><span class="down">Down</span></td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td></tr><tr><td>Time to expiration</td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td></tr><tr><td>Volatility</td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td></tr><tr><td>Interest rates</td><td><span class="up">Up, a little</span></td><td><span class="down">Down, a little</span></td></tr><tr><td>Expected dividends</td><td><span class="down">Down</span></td><td><span class="up">Up</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Notice the pattern. Price, strike, rates and dividends push calls and puts in <em>opposite</em> directions. Time and volatility push both the <em>same</em> way, because more time and bigger swings mean more possibility, and possibility is what every option buyer pays for.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each change to its effect on a call option's price.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Stock price rises', right: 'Call price rises' },
          { left: 'Higher expected dividend', right: 'Call price falls' },
          { left: 'Volatility jumps', right: 'Call price rises' },
          { left: 'Picking a higher strike', right: 'Call costs less' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Price, strike and time', html: `<p><strong>Stock price and strike</strong> work together through moneyness. The further a call is in the money, the more intrinsic value it has and the more it costs. Same for puts in the other direction.</p><p><strong>Time</strong> is possibility. A 90-day option gives the stock three months to make its move. A 7-day option gives it one week. More time almost always means a higher price for both calls and puts. For American-style options, extra time can never hurt, since you can always exercise early.</p><p>One subtle point: doubling the time does <em>not</em> double the price. As you will see in the theta lesson, an at-the-money option's time value grows roughly with the <em>square root</em> of time. A 120-day option is not four times the price of a 30-day option. Closer to twice.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Volatility: the input nobody can see', html: `<p>Here is the twist. Stock price, strike, time, rates and dividends are all known numbers you can look up. <strong>Volatility</strong> is different. It is about how much the stock <em>will</em> swing between now and expiration, and nobody knows the future.</p><p>Picture two stocks, both at $100. One is a sleepy utility that moves about 1% on a wild day. The other is a small biotech waiting on a drug approval decision next week. Would you charge the same for a 110 call on both?</p><p>Of course not. The biotech might jump 40% or crash 60%. Because an option buyer's downside stops at the premium, wild swings help them: big gains count fully, big losses are capped. So <span class="hl">higher expected volatility means higher option prices</span>, for calls and puts alike.</p><p>Since nobody can measure future volatility, the market works backward from option prices to see how much volatility traders are assuming. That number is called <strong>implied volatility</strong>, and it gets the whole next lesson.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Five inputs you can look up. One you have to guess. Guess which one causes all the arguments.` } },
        { type: 'check', question: `Two stocks both trade at $100. Which 110 call, with the same expiration, is likely to cost more?`, options: [`The call on a stable utility stock`, `The call on a biotech awaiting a major drug decision`, `They cost the same because the strike is the same`, `The utility, because it pays a dividend`], answer: 1, explain: `The biotech is expected to swing far more, which raises the odds of a big move above $110. Higher expected volatility means a higher premium.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Rates and dividends: the quiet inputs', html: `<p><strong>Interest rates.</strong> Buying a call instead of 100 shares leaves most of your cash free to earn interest. When rates are higher, that perk is worth more, so calls get slightly more valuable and puts slightly less. For options expiring in a few weeks, the effect is tiny. For options a year or two out, it can matter.</p><p><strong>Dividends.</strong> When a stock goes ex-dividend, its price typically drops by about the dividend amount, because the cash has left the company. Call holders do not get the dividend, so an expected payout is a known drop that hurts calls and helps puts. A stock about to pay a large dividend will have slightly cheaper calls and slightly pricier puts than an identical stock that pays nothing.</p><p>For most short-term trades, these two are background noise compared with price, time and volatility. But they explain small price differences that otherwise look like mistakes.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A larger expected dividend before expiration makes a stock's call options more expensive.`, answer: false, explain: `The stock price is expected to drop by about the dividend on the ex-date, and call holders do not receive it. That lowers call values and raises put values.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Black-Scholes in plain English', html: `<p>In 1973, Fischer Black and Myron Scholes published a formula for pricing options, building on work with Robert Merton. Scholes and Merton received the 1997 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for it. Black had died in 1995, and the prize is not awarded after death.</p><p>The big idea fits in three steps:</p><ol><li>Imagine every price the stock could reach by expiration, with more likely prices weighted more heavily. Volatility sets how wide that spread of outcomes is.</li><li>For each possible price, work out what the option would pay. Below the strike, a call pays zero.</li><li>Average those payoffs and discount the result back to today.</li></ol><p>Widen the spread of outcomes and the average payoff rises, because the big wins count while the big losses are floored at zero. That is the math behind "more volatility, more expensive options."</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: `Model inputs: price, strike, time, volatility, rate, dividends. Output: a fair-value estimate. Estimate is the key word.` } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Models are maps, not the territory', html: `<p>Black-Scholes assumes volatility stays constant and prices move smoothly, with no sudden gaps. Real stocks gap on earnings, crash in panics and calm down for months. Traders adjust by using different implied volatilities for different strikes. For stock indexes, out-of-the-money puts usually trade at higher implied volatility than out-of-the-money calls, a pattern called <strong>skew</strong>, because investors pay up for crash protection. A model price is a starting point, not a guarantee.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'Calls and puts are tied together', html: `<p>For European-style options with the same strike and expiration, the call price minus the put price is roughly equal to the stock price minus the strike, adjusted a bit for interest and dividends. This link is called <strong>put-call parity</strong>. If prices drift away from it, traders can lock in a near riskless profit, and their trades pull prices back in line. For LMNB at $50, that is why the 50 call and 50 put trade at nearly the same price.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: `Same stock, same day. Order these calls from cheapest to most expensive.`, items: [`60 strike, 30 days left`, `55 strike, 30 days left`, `55 strike, 90 days left`, `50 strike, 90 days left`], explain: `A higher strike makes a call cheaper, and more time makes it pricier. The 60 strike with 30 days is the least likely to pay off, and the 50 strike with 90 days is the most valuable.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Which input pushes BOTH call and put prices up when it rises?`, options: [`Stock price`, `Interest rates`, `Volatility`, `Dividends`], answer: 2, explain: `Higher volatility means larger possible moves in either direction, which helps both calls and puts.` },
          { q: `Why is volatility different from the other pricing inputs?`, options: [`It is set by the exchange`, `It never changes`, `It only affects puts`, `Future volatility cannot be observed, so it must be estimated`], answer: 3, explain: `Price, strike, time, rates and dividends are known. How much the stock will swing is a forecast.` },
          { q: `Rising interest rates generally make calls...`, options: [`Slightly more valuable`, `Slightly less valuable`, `Worthless`, `Unaffected in every case`], answer: 0, explain: `A call ties up less cash than buying the stock, and that saved cash earns more interest when rates are higher.` },
          { q: `In the Black-Scholes intuition, why does a wider spread of possible outcomes raise a call's value?`, options: [`Because stocks always rise over time`, `Big gains count fully while losses below the strike are capped at zero payoff`, `Because the strike moves with volatility`, `Because sellers pay the buyer interest`], answer: 1, explain: `The option payoff is lopsided. More extreme outcomes add upside without adding matching downside.` },
          { q: `For stock index options, out-of-the-money puts usually trade at higher implied volatility than out-of-the-money calls. This is called...`, options: [`Put-call parity`, `Theta`, `Skew`, `Contango`], answer: 2, explain: `Skew reflects strong demand for downside protection.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'options-2-implied-volatility',
      title: 'Implied Volatility & IV Crush',
      summary: `Implied volatility is the market's guess at future swings, baked into every option price. Learn to read it, rank it, and survive the post-earnings crush.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'fire',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two kinds of volatility', html: `<p>Volatility measures how much a price swings. Traders talk about two versions:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="How much a stock actually moved over a past period, measured as the annualized standard deviation of its returns.">Historical volatility (HV)</span> looks backward. It measures how much the stock actually moved over, say, the last 30 days.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="The volatility level that, plugged into a pricing model, produces the option's current market price. The market's forecast of future swings.">Implied volatility (IV)</span> looks forward. It is the volatility number that makes a pricing model spit out the option's current market price.</li></ul><p>Both are quoted as an <strong>annualized percentage</strong>. An IV of 30% means the option market is pricing in yearly swings with a standard deviation of about 30%.</p><p>The key insight: <span class="hl">IV is just option price in a different unit</span>. When options get more expensive with everything else unchanged, IV rises. When they get cheaper, IV falls. Saying "IV is high" is the same as saying "options are pricey right now."</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `Historical volatility is the rearview mirror. Implied volatility is the weather forecast. Both useful, neither perfect.` } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Volatility vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Historical volatility', back: `How much the stock actually moved in the past, annualized.` },
          { front: 'Implied volatility', back: `How much movement the option prices are assuming for the future.` },
          { front: 'IV rank', back: `Where today's IV sits between its 52-week low (0) and high (100).` },
          { front: 'IV crush', back: `A sharp drop in IV after an uncertain event passes, shrinking option prices.` },
          { front: 'VIX', back: `An index of 30-day implied volatility calculated from S&P 500 options.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The rule of 16', html: `<p>An annual IV number is hard to feel. Here is a shortcut traders use. There are about 252 trading days in a year, and the square root of 252 is close to 16. So:</p><p><span class="hl">Expected daily move is about IV divided by 16</span></p><p>If LMNB has an IV of <strong>32%</strong>, the options are pricing a typical daily move of about 32 / 16 = <strong>2%</strong>. On a $50 stock, that is about $1 a day, up or down.</p><p>"Typical" here means one standard deviation. In a textbook bell curve, about two-thirds of days fall inside that range. Real markets have fatter tails than the textbook, though: big surprise days happen more often than a bell curve predicts. Treat the rule as a quick sense check, not a promise.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: `Square root of 252 trading days is about 15.9. Round to 16 and you can do this in your head on the bus.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A stock's options show an implied volatility of 48%. Using the rule of 16, about how big a daily move (in percent) are they pricing in?`, answer: 3, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: `48 divided by 16 is 3. The options imply a typical daily move of about 3%.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Is this IV high or low?', html: `<p>An IV of 40% is wild for a giant consumer-staples company and sleepy for a tiny biotech. To judge IV, compare a stock to <em>its own</em> history.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Where current IV sits in its 52-week range, from 0 (the low) to 100 (the high).">IV rank</span> does exactly that:</p><p><span class="hl">IV rank = (current IV - 52-week low) / (52-week high - 52-week low) x 100</span></p><p>If LMNB's IV is 45%, and over the past year it ranged from 25% to 65%: (45 - 25) / (65 - 25) x 100 = <strong>50</strong>. Smack in the middle of its range.</p><p>A cousin, <strong>IV percentile</strong>, counts the share of days in the past year when IV was lower than today. The two can differ a lot if one extreme spike stretched the range. Brokers and charting tools display one or both, and their exact methods vary.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A stock's IV is 30% today. Over the past year it ranged from a low of 20% to a high of 60%. What is its IV rank?`, answer: 25, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '', explain: `(30 - 20) / (60 - 20) x 100 = 10 / 40 x 100 = 25. IV is in the lower quarter of its yearly range.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The earnings run-up', html: `<p>Some events are scheduled: earnings reports, product launches, drug approval decisions, central bank meetings. Everyone knows a big move <em>might</em> happen on that day, just not which way.</p><p>So in the days before, option prices on that stock usually climb, especially for expirations right after the event. IV rises because traders are paying for the possibility of a jump. You will often see the nearest expiration with a much higher IV than options expiring a month later.</p><p>Traders often estimate the market's <strong>expected move</strong> for the event from the price of the at-the-money call plus the at-the-money put (a straddle). If that combo costs $6 on a $100 stock, the market is pricing roughly a $6 move, either way. That is a rough rule of thumb, not an exact forecast.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'iv-crush', caption: `IV climbs into a scheduled event, then collapses right after it. The drop in IV wipes out much of the option's extrinsic value overnight.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'IV crush: right and still wrong', html: `<p>Dev is sure LMNB will beat earnings. The stock is <strong>$100</strong>. The night before the report, he buys a 100 call expiring that Friday for <strong>$6.00</strong> ($600). IV is sky high.</p><p>Earnings are good. LMNB opens at <strong>$103</strong>, up 3%. Dev celebrates for about four seconds.</p><p>The uncertainty is gone, so IV collapses. His call is now worth about <strong>$3.60</strong>: $3 of intrinsic value plus $0.60 of leftover time value. He was right about direction and still lost <span class="down">$240</span>.</p><p>That is <span class="term" data-def="The sharp fall in implied volatility, and therefore option prices, after an anticipated event is over.">IV crush</span>. The market had priced in about a $6 move. A $3 move was a disappointment, as far as the option was concerned. To win buying options into earnings, you need the stock to move <em>more than the market expected</em>, not just in the right direction.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Buying options right before earnings means paying peak prices for uncertainty that disappears overnight. Know the expected move first.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Dev paid $6.00 for one call before earnings. After the report, the call is worth $3.60. How many dollars did he lose on the contract?`, answer: 240, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$6.00 - $3.60 = $2.40 per share, times 100 shares = $240.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Who wins from the crush?', html: `<p>If buyers get hurt by IV crush, sellers must benefit, right? Often, yes. Someone who sold Dev that call at $6.00 and bought it back at $3.60 made $240.</p><p>But this is not a free lunch for sellers either. The market priced a $6 move because $6 moves, and sometimes $20 moves, really do happen. When a company badly misses or gets a surprise buyout offer, short option positions can take a loss many times larger than the premium they collected. Over many events, sellers win small amounts often and lose big amounts occasionally.</p><p>The honest takeaway: option prices before an event already reflect a lot of information. Beating them requires being right about the <em>size</em> of the move compared with what is priced in, which is much harder than guessing direction.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Look before you buy', html: `<p>Before buying any option, check two things on the chain: its IV, and the IV of the same stock's options a month or two further out. If the nearest expiration has a much higher IV, an event is probably priced in. That does not make buying wrong, but it tells you the price includes a hefty fee for uncertainty that will vanish the moment the news is out.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `When is IV crush most likely to hit an option's price?`, options: [`The week a company is added to a stock index`, `Right after a scheduled earnings announcement`, `On the day an option is first listed`, `Whenever the stock pays a dividend`], answer: 1, explain: `IV rises ahead of a known event and drops sharply once the news is out and the uncertainty is gone.` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A high implied volatility tells you the stock is more likely to go down than up.`, answer: false, explain: `IV measures the expected size of moves, not their direction. High IV means bigger expected swings either way.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Implied volatility is best described as...`, options: [`How much the stock moved last month`, `The volatility the current option price is assuming for the future`, `The exchange's official risk rating`, `The average daily trading volume`], answer: 1, explain: `IV is backed out of option prices. It is the market's forecast of future movement.` },
          { q: `A stock's IV is 64%. Using the rule of 16, what daily move is priced in?`, options: [`About 1%`, `About 6.4%`, `About 16%`, `About 4%`], answer: 3, explain: `64 / 16 = 4, so about a 4% typical daily move.` },
          { q: `IV is 50%, the 52-week low is 30% and the high is 80%. IV rank?`, options: [`40`, `50`, `62.5`, `20`], answer: 0, explain: `(50 - 30) / (80 - 30) x 100 = 20 / 50 x 100 = 40.` },
          { q: `Ms. Ortiz buys a call the day before earnings. The stock rises 2% after the report, but her call loses value. The most likely reason?`, options: [`The strike changed`, `The stock paid a dividend`, `IV crush outweighed the small gain in the stock`, `Calls always lose money after earnings`], answer: 2, explain: `The market expected a bigger move. When uncertainty vanished, IV fell, and the drop in extrinsic value beat the small price gain.` },
          { q: `The VIX is calculated from...`, options: [`S&P 500 index option prices`, `The 30 largest stocks' earnings`, `Bond yields`, `Historical daily returns of the Dow`], answer: 0, explain: `The VIX measures 30-day implied volatility from S&P 500 index options.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'options-2-delta-gamma',
      title: 'Delta & Gamma: Speed and Acceleration',
      summary: `Delta tells you how much an option moves when the stock moves. Gamma tells you how fast delta itself changes. Together they explain why options feel so jumpy.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Meet the Greeks', html: `<p>Option traders use Greek letters to describe how an option's price reacts to each input you learned about. They are called <span class="term" data-def="Measures of how an option's price responds to changes in stock price, time, volatility and interest rates.">the Greeks</span>:</p><ul><li><strong>Delta:</strong> reaction to the stock price</li><li><strong>Gamma:</strong> how fast delta changes</li><li><strong>Theta:</strong> reaction to time passing</li><li><strong>Vega:</strong> reaction to implied volatility</li><li><strong>Rho:</strong> reaction to interest rates</li></ul><p>(Vega is not actually a Greek letter. Traders adopted it anyway.)</p><p>Most broker apps show the Greeks right on the option chain. They come from pricing models, so they are estimates, and they change every time the stock, the clock or IV moves. This lesson covers the two that deal with stock price: delta and gamma.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `If an option were a car: delta is your speed, gamma is how hard you are pressing the gas.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'greeks', caption: `Each Greek measures sensitivity to one input. Delta and gamma track the stock price, theta tracks time, vega tracks volatility and rho tracks rates.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Delta: dollars per dollar', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="About how much an option's price changes for a $1 move in the underlying.">Delta</span> estimates how much an option's price changes when the stock moves $1.</p><p>Leo's LMNB 55 call costs $2.00 and has a delta of <strong>0.40</strong>. If LMNB rises $1, the call should rise about $0.40, to roughly $2.40. That is <span class="up">+$40</span> per contract. If LMNB falls $1, the call drops to roughly $1.60.</p><p>Call deltas run from <strong>0 to 1</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Deep in the money:</strong> close to 1. Moves almost like the stock.</li><li><strong>At the money:</strong> around 0.50.</li><li><strong>Far out of the money:</strong> close to 0. Barely reacts to small moves.</li></ul><p>That matches what you learned about moneyness. The deep ITM 40 call acts like stock. The far OTM 60 call shrugs at a $1 move.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Puts have negative delta', html: `<p>Puts gain value when the stock falls, so their deltas are <strong>negative</strong>, running from <strong>0 to -1</strong>. An at-the-money put has a delta of about -0.50.</p><p>Dev's 45 put has a delta of <strong>-0.30</strong>. If LMNB drops $1, the put gains about $0.30. If LMNB rises $1, it loses about $0.30.</p><p>Selling flips the sign. If you are <em>short</em> an option, your delta is the opposite of the buyer's:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Delta sign</th><th>Wants the stock to...</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Long call</td><td>Positive</td><td>Rise</td></tr><tr><td>Short call</td><td>Negative</td><td>Fall or stay below strike</td></tr><tr><td>Long put</td><td>Negative</td><td>Fall</td></tr><tr><td>Short put</td><td>Positive</td><td>Rise or stay above strike</td></tr></tbody></table>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A put has a delta of -0.35. If the stock rises $2, about how many dollars does one contract lose?`, answer: 70, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: `-0.35 x $2 = -$0.70 per share, times 100 shares, so the put loses about $70.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Delta as share equivalents', html: `<p>Because one contract covers 100 shares, delta also tells you how many shares your position acts like. A call with delta 0.40 behaves roughly like owning <strong>40 shares</strong>.</p><p>Leo owns 2 calls with delta 0.60 each: 2 x 0.60 x 100 = <span class="hl">120 share equivalents</span>. If the stock rises $1, he gains about $120, the same as if he held 120 shares.</p><p>This is how market makers think. When they sell you a call, they usually buy some stock to offset the delta, aiming for a <span class="term" data-def="A position whose total delta is near zero, so small stock moves barely change its value.">delta-neutral</span> book. They want to earn the spread, not bet on direction. You can use the same idea to compare positions: is this trade like owning 20 shares or 200?</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz owns 3 calls, each with a delta of 0.25. How many shares of stock does her position act like?`, answer: 75, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '', explain: `3 contracts x 0.25 x 100 shares = 75 share equivalents.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Delta as a rough probability', html: `<p>Many traders use delta as a quick, rough estimate of the chance an option finishes in the money. A 0.30 delta call is treated as having <em>roughly</em> a 30% chance of expiring in the money. An at-the-money option at about 0.50 is close to a coin flip.</p><p>This is a rule of thumb, not an exact probability. The real model math uses a slightly different number, and the estimate depends on the implied volatility being right. Still, it is a useful gut check. If you are buying a 0.10 delta call, the market is saying it will expire worthless most of the time.</p><p>It also tells you something about sellers. Someone selling a 0.15 delta put is usually going to keep the premium, but on the occasions they are wrong, the loss can be large.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Delta near 0.10 means the market sees roughly one chance in ten of finishing in the money. Cheap options are cheap for a reason.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Gamma: how fast delta changes', html: `<p>Delta does not stay put. As the stock rises, a call gets closer to (or deeper into) the money, and its delta grows. <span class="term" data-def="How much an option's delta changes for a $1 move in the underlying.">Gamma</span> measures that change.</p><p>Leo's 50 call has delta <strong>0.50</strong> and gamma <strong>0.06</strong>. If LMNB rises $1, delta climbs to about 0.56. Another $1, about 0.62. His call speeds up as it wins. If LMNB falls $1, delta drops to about 0.44, so it loses value more slowly as it loses.</p><p>That is why option buyers are said to be <strong>long gamma</strong>: moves in your favor accelerate, moves against you decelerate. Option sellers are <strong>short gamma</strong>: the move against them speeds up. A short call that started at -0.30 delta can become -0.80 delta after a big rally, losing almost dollar for dollar with the stock.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Gamma near expiration', html: `<p>Gamma is highest for <strong>at-the-money</strong> options, and it gets dramatically bigger as expiration approaches.</p><p>With months left, an ATM option's delta drifts gradually as the stock moves. With one day left, the same option is a hair trigger. A small move can swing delta from 0.20 to 0.80, because in a few hours the option will be worth either something or nothing.</p><p>This is a big reason why options expiring the same day can double or go to zero within minutes. It is also why sellers of near-expiration ATM options can see small losses snowball. High gamma means your position's risk can change faster than you can react.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `High gamma near expiration turns a calm position into a roller coaster. Sellers feel it most, and fastest.` } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Dev checks his real exposure', html: `<p>Dev owns 10 far OTM calls with delta 0.08 each and thinks he is "all in." His position acts like just 10 x 0.08 x 100 = 80 shares. A $1 rally earns him about $80. But if the stock rips higher, gamma kicks in, deltas grow, and that same position can start acting like hundreds of shares. Delta tells you where you are today, gamma tells you how fast that can change.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Which option would usually have the highest gamma?`, options: [`A deep in-the-money call with 6 months left`, `A far out-of-the-money put with 1 year left`, `An at-the-money call expiring tomorrow`, `A deep in-the-money put expiring tomorrow`], answer: 2, explain: `Gamma peaks at the money and grows as expiration nears. Deep ITM or far OTM options have deltas pinned near 1 or 0, so delta has little room to change.` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each option to its approximate delta.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Deep in-the-money call', right: 'Close to +1' },
          { left: 'Far out-of-the-money call', right: 'Close to 0' },
          { left: 'At-the-money put', right: 'About -0.50' },
          { left: 'Deep in-the-money put', right: 'Close to -1' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A call has delta 0.65. The stock rises $2. About how much does one contract gain?`, options: [`$65`, `$130`, `$1.30`, `$200`], answer: 1, explain: `0.65 x $2 = $1.30 per share, times 100 = $130.` },
          { q: `What is the delta sign of a short put?`, options: [`Negative`, `Zero`, `It depends on the strike only`, `Positive`], answer: 3, explain: `A long put has negative delta. Selling flips it to positive: the short put benefits when the stock rises.` },
          { q: `Leo owns 4 puts with delta -0.40. His position acts like...`, options: [`Short 160 shares`, `Long 160 shares`, `Short 40 shares`, `Long 400 shares`], answer: 0, explain: `4 x -0.40 x 100 = -160, which behaves like being short 160 shares.` },
          { q: `A call has delta 0.45 and gamma 0.05. The stock rises $1. Its new delta is about...`, options: [`0.40`, `0.45`, `0.50`, `0.95`], answer: 2, explain: `Gamma adds about 0.05 to delta for each $1 rise: 0.45 + 0.05 = 0.50.` },
          { q: `Why are option sellers said to be short gamma?`, options: [`They pay the premium`, `Moves against their position speed up as delta grows`, `They cannot be assigned`, `Their delta never changes`], answer: 1, explain: `As the stock moves against a short option, its delta grows larger, so losses accelerate.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'options-2-theta-decay',
      title: 'Theta: The Melting Ice Cube',
      summary: `Every option loses a little value each day just from time passing. See why that decay speeds up near expiration and who collects it.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'clock',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Time is a cost', html: `<p>Remember extrinsic value, the part of an option's price that pays for possibility? It shrinks every day, because every day there is less time left for something to happen. By expiration, it is gone.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="About how much an option's price drops from one day passing, with everything else unchanged.">Theta</span> measures that daily shrinkage. A long option with a theta of <strong>-0.05</strong> loses about $0.05 per share per day, or <span class="down">$5 per contract</span>, if the stock and IV do not change.</p><p>Traders call options a <strong>melting ice cube</strong>. If you buy one, you are holding something that gets smaller while you wait. The stock has to move enough, soon enough, to make up for the melt.</p><p>Stocks do not have this problem. If you buy a share and the stock goes nowhere for a year, you still have the share. If you buy an option and the stock goes nowhere, you lose the extrinsic value.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `Owning an option is like renting a possibility. Rent is due every single day, weekends included.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'time-decay', caption: `Time value drains slowly when expiration is far away, then drops off a cliff in the final weeks. The curve is steepest right at the end.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Decay is not a straight line', html: `<p>Theta is not constant. For an at-the-money option, decay <strong>speeds up</strong> as expiration approaches.</p><p>A handy simplified model: an ATM option's time value grows with the <em>square root</em> of the time left. Four times the time is worth about twice the price, not four times. Run it backward and you see the cliff. An idealized ATM option worth $4.00 with 64 days left:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Days left</th><th>Approx. value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>64</td><td>$4.00</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>$3.00</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>$2.00</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>$1.00</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>$0.50</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first dollar of value took 28 days to melt. The third dollar melted in just 12 days. The final 50 cents vanishes in one day. Real options do not follow this perfectly, but the shape is right.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Using the square-root rule, an at-the-money option is worth $3.00 with 100 days left. Roughly what is it worth with 25 days left, if nothing else changes?`, answer: 1.5, tolerance: 0.05, unit: '$', explain: `25 days is one quarter of 100. The square root of one quarter is one half, so the value is about $3.00 x 0.5 = $1.50.` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'theta-decay', props: { premium: 3, days: 60 }, caption: `Slide the days remaining toward zero and watch how slowly the value melts at first, then how fast it collapses near expiration.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Which options melt fastest?', html: `<p>Decay depends on moneyness too.</p><ul><li><strong>At the money:</strong> the most extrinsic value, so the most dollars melt each day. The end-of-life cliff is sharpest here.</li><li><strong>Out of the money:</strong> fewer dollars of decay, but the option is all extrinsic. In percentage terms it can melt very fast near expiration, often heading to $0.</li><li><strong>Deep in the money:</strong> mostly intrinsic value, which does not decay. Theta is small.</li></ul><p>This is why short-dated OTM options are so brutal for buyers. The option is 100% time value with little time left. If the stock does not move fast, the whole premium evaporates, sometimes in a few days.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Who pays and who collects', html: `<p>Time decay is a transfer. Every dollar of extrinsic value that a buyer loses to the clock ends up with the seller.</p><ul><li><strong>Option buyers</strong> have <strong>negative theta</strong>. Time works against them.</li><li><strong>Option sellers</strong> have <strong>positive theta</strong>. Time works for them, and each quiet day is a small win.</li></ul><p>That sounds like sellers have the easy job. But theta and gamma come as a pair. The seller collects theta <em>because</em> they are short gamma: if the stock makes a big, fast move, their losses accelerate. Buyers pay theta <em>for</em> long gamma: the chance of explosive gains.</p><p>Neither side is free money. Sellers get paid steadily for taking the risk of rare big losses. Buyers pay steadily for the chance of rare big wins.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Selling options is often described as picking up nickels in front of a steamroller. The nickels are real. So is the steamroller.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz owns 5 call contracts, each with a theta of -0.04. Roughly how many dollars of value will they lose over 10 days from time decay alone, assuming theta stays the same?`, answer: 200, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: `0.04 x 100 shares x 5 contracts = $20 per day. Over 10 days, about $200. In reality theta usually grows as expiration nears, so the actual loss could be larger.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Weekends and holidays', html: `<p>Time passes on weekends, so options lose value over weekends too. But do not expect a magic Monday drop. Market makers know the weekend is coming, and option prices on Thursday and Friday usually already reflect it. Much of the weekend decay is priced in before the Friday close.</p><p>Some brokers show theta based on calendar days and some on trading days, which is one reason Greeks can differ slightly from app to app.</p><p>The bigger lesson: no matter which day you look, a long option is quietly paying rent. If you buy an option and then plan to "wait and see" for a few weeks, the waiting has a price. Know roughly what it is before you start.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Time decay runs on the calendar, but prices are set by humans who can read calendars. The weekend is rarely a surprise.` } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Time decay terms', cards: [
          { front: 'Theta', back: `The estimated dollar drop in an option's price per share from one day passing.` },
          { front: 'Negative theta', back: `Time hurts you. Every long option position has it.` },
          { front: 'Positive theta', back: `Time helps you. Option sellers collect it.` },
          { front: 'Time value', back: `Another name for extrinsic value, the only part of the price that decays.` },
          { front: 'Expiration cliff', back: `The steep drop in time value during an option's final weeks, sharpest at the money.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: selling options is passive income', html: `<p>Collecting theta every day can feel like a paycheck. It is not. It is payment for carrying a risk, the way an insurance company earns premiums only because it sometimes pays huge claims. A seller can collect small amounts for months and give it all back, and more, in one sharp move. Steady income and low risk are not the same thing.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `An at-the-money option loses roughly the same dollar amount of time value every day of its life.`, answer: false, explain: `Decay accelerates. An ATM option loses little per day when expiration is far off and much more in its final weeks.` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Maya compares two at-the-money calls on the same stock: one with 7 days left and one with 60 days left. Which one will lose a larger percentage of its value tomorrow, if nothing else changes?`, options: [`The 60-day call`, `The 7-day call`, `They lose the same percentage`, `Neither, because theta only applies after a week`], answer: 1, explain: `Decay is steepest near expiration. The 7-day option loses a much bigger slice of its remaining value each day.` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Buying time on purpose', html: `<p>Many option buyers choose more time than they think the move will need, then plan to exit before the last few weeks, when decay is steepest. It costs more up front, but the daily melt is slower and a delay in the move is less likely to wipe out the trade. Sellers often do the reverse, focusing on shorter windows where decay is fastest, and accept the gamma risk that comes with it.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A long option has a theta of -0.08. About how much does one contract lose per day from time decay?`, options: [`$0.08`, `$0.80`, `$8`, `$80`], answer: 2, explain: `$0.08 per share x 100 shares = $8 per day, all else equal.` },
          { q: `Who has positive theta?`, options: [`Option sellers`, `Call buyers only`, `Put buyers only`, `Nobody`], answer: 0, explain: `Sellers collect the extrinsic value that buyers lose as time passes.` },
          { q: `When does an at-the-money option's time decay speed up most?`, options: [`Right after it is listed`, `About six months before expiration`, `On dividend dates`, `In the last few weeks before expiration`], answer: 3, explain: `The decay curve steepens sharply near expiration.` },
          { q: `Using the square-root rule, an ATM option with 9 times as much time left costs about...`, options: [`9 times as much`, `3 times as much`, `The same`, `Half as much`], answer: 1, explain: `The square root of 9 is 3, so roughly three times the time value.` },
          { q: `Why is collecting theta not free money for sellers?`, options: [`Theta is always zero`, `Sellers must pay theta to the exchange`, `Sellers are short gamma, so big fast moves can cause large losses`, `Theta only helps buyers`], answer: 2, explain: `Positive theta comes paired with negative gamma. Sellers earn decay in exchange for the risk of sharp moves.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'options-2-vega-rho-together',
      title: 'Vega, Rho & the Full Picture',
      summary: `Vega tracks volatility, rho tracks interest rates. Then we combine all five Greeks to estimate how a real position changes overnight.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Vega: the volatility dial', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="About how much an option's price changes for a 1 percentage point change in implied volatility.">Vega</span> estimates how much an option's price changes when implied volatility moves by <strong>1 percentage point</strong>, for example from 30% to 31%.</p><p>A call with a vega of <strong>0.12</strong> gains about $0.12 per share if IV rises one point, and loses about $0.12 if IV falls one point. If IV drops 10 points after earnings, that is about $1.20 per share, or <span class="down">-$120</span> per contract, even if the stock does not move.</p><p>Both long calls and long puts have <strong>positive vega</strong>, because higher volatility makes all options more valuable. Sellers have <strong>negative vega</strong>: they benefit when IV falls and get hurt when it spikes.</p><p>Now you can describe IV crush in Greek: a long option with big vega meets a big drop in IV.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Vega answers one question: if the market gets more nervous or calmer, what happens to my option?` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `An option has a vega of 0.08. Implied volatility rises 5 percentage points, and nothing else changes. About how many dollars does one contract gain?`, answer: 40, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `0.08 x 5 = $0.40 per share, times 100 shares = $40.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Where vega is biggest', html: `<p>Vega is largest for <strong>at-the-money options with lots of time left</strong>. A two-year option has many months for volatility to matter, so a change in IV moves its price a lot. An option expiring tomorrow has tiny vega: there is barely any time for volatility to play out.</p><p>Notice the trade-off with gamma. Short-dated options have high gamma and low vega: they react violently to the stock price. Long-dated options have low gamma and high vega: they react more to changes in IV.</p><p>One wrinkle: around events like earnings, the IV of the nearest expiration usually swings far more than the IV of later months. So a short-dated option can still get hit hard by IV crush, because its IV falls by many more points, even though its vega is smaller.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Rho: the sleepy Greek', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="About how much an option's price changes for a 1 percentage point change in interest rates.">Rho</span> measures sensitivity to interest rates, per 1 percentage point change. Calls have <strong>positive rho</strong> and puts have <strong>negative rho</strong>, for the reason you saw earlier: owning a call instead of shares frees up cash that earns interest.</p><p>For short-dated options, rho is so small that most traders ignore it. Interest rates rarely move a full point in a few weeks, and even when they do, a one-month option barely notices.</p><p>For <span class="term" data-def="Long-term equity anticipation securities: listed options that expire more than a year out.">LEAPS</span> it matters more. A one-year at-the-money call on a $50 stock might have a rho of about 0.24. If rates rise one full point, that call gains roughly $0.24, or $24 per contract. Not huge, but not nothing, and it grows with longer expirations and pricier stocks.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Rho naps through most short-term trades. Wake it up with a long-dated option and a surprise rate move.` } },
        { type: 'check', question: `Which option is most sensitive to a change in interest rates?`, options: [`A call expiring this Friday`, `A 2-year LEAPS call`, `A put expiring in 2 weeks`, `All options are equally sensitive`], answer: 1, explain: `Rho grows with time to expiration. Long-dated options feel rate changes the most.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'All five Greeks on one table', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Delta</th><th>Gamma</th><th>Theta</th><th>Vega</th><th>Rho</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Long call</td><td>+</td><td>+</td><td>-</td><td>+</td><td>+</td></tr><tr><td>Long put</td><td>-</td><td>+</td><td>-</td><td>+</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Short call</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>+</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Short put</td><td>+</td><td>-</td><td>+</td><td>-</td><td>+</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two patterns are worth memorizing. First, every <strong>long</strong> option has positive gamma, negative theta and positive vega: you pay time for movement and volatility. Every <strong>short</strong> option is the reverse.</p><p>Second, delta and rho depend on call or put. Calls like the stock and rates rising. Puts like them falling.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each Greek to what it measures.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Delta', right: 'Change from a $1 stock move' },
          { left: 'Gamma', right: 'Change in delta from a $1 stock move' },
          { left: 'Theta', right: 'Change from one day passing' },
          { left: 'Vega', right: 'Change from a 1-point IV move' },
          { left: 'Rho', right: 'Change from a 1-point rate move' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Worked example: Leo overnight', html: `<p>Leo owns one LMNB 55 call worth <strong>$2.00</strong>. His app shows: delta 0.40, gamma 0.05, theta -0.04, vega 0.07.</p><p>Overnight, LMNB rises <strong>$1</strong> and IV falls <strong>2 points</strong>. Estimate the new price, one Greek at a time:</p><ul><li><strong>Delta:</strong> +0.40 x $1 = +$0.40</li><li><strong>Gamma bonus:</strong> delta rose during the move, adding about half of 0.05, so roughly +$0.03</li><li><strong>Theta:</strong> one day passed, -$0.04</li><li><strong>Vega:</strong> 0.07 x -2 = -$0.14</li></ul><p>Total: about +$0.25 per share. The call is worth roughly <strong>$2.25</strong>, a gain of about <span class="up">$25</span> per contract.</p><p>The stock went up $1, which "should" have been worth $40, but time and falling IV ate over a third of it. This is how the Greeks turn a vague feeling of "my option did not go up as much as I expected" into specific reasons.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Same call as Leo's: theta -0.04, vega 0.07. One day passes, the stock does not move, and IV rises 3 points. About how many dollars does one contract gain?`, answer: 17, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: `Theta: -$0.04. Vega: 0.07 x 3 = +$0.21. Net +$0.17 per share, times 100 = about $17.` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A short put position has positive delta.`, answer: true, explain: `A long put has negative delta. Selling it flips the sign, so a short put gains when the stock rises.` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: `Two of Dev's calls, same news`, html: `<p>Dev owns a weekly LMNB call and a two-year LEAPS call, both at the money. One morning the stock jumps $2 and IV drops 5 points. The weekly, with high gamma and little vega, mostly feels the price jump and gains nicely. The LEAPS, with lower gamma and big vega, gains much less because the IV drop eats into the price move. Same stock, same morning, very different results, all predictable from the Greeks.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Greek quick reference', cards: [
          { front: 'Delta', back: `Price change per $1 move in the stock. Calls positive, puts negative.` },
          { front: 'Gamma', back: `How fast delta changes. Biggest at the money near expiration.` },
          { front: 'Theta', back: `Daily time decay. Hurts buyers, helps sellers.` },
          { front: 'Vega', back: `Change per 1-point IV move. Biggest for long-dated, at-the-money options.` },
          { front: 'Rho', back: `Change per 1-point rate move. Matters most for long-dated options.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Greeks are estimates, not promises', html: `<p>The Greeks are snapshots from a model. They are very good for small changes over short periods. They get less reliable when:</p><ul><li><strong>The move is big.</strong> Delta and gamma describe the next dollar, not the next twenty. An earnings gap can blow straight past the estimate.</li><li><strong>Time passes.</strong> Every Greek changes as the days tick by, especially near expiration.</li><li><strong>The model's assumptions break.</strong> Real IV is not constant, and different apps use slightly different models and inputs, so their Greeks rarely match perfectly.</li></ul><p>Use them like a weather forecast: a solid guide for planning, with the understanding that storms can arrive anyway. The most useful habit is checking your position's total delta, theta and vega before you trade, so you know what you are actually betting on.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Greeks describe small, calm moves. Markets do not always move small or calm. Size your positions for the storm, not the forecast.` } },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A long put has which vega sign?`, options: [`Positive`, `Negative`, `Zero`, `Same as a short call`], answer: 0, explain: `All long options benefit from rising implied volatility, so long puts have positive vega.` },
          { q: `A call has vega 0.15. IV falls 8 points. About how much does one contract lose from vega alone?`, options: [`$15`, `$80`, `$8`, `$120`], answer: 3, explain: `0.15 x 8 = $1.20 per share, times 100 = $120.` },
          { q: `Which statement about rho is true?`, options: [`Puts have positive rho`, `Rho matters most for long-dated options`, `Rho is the biggest Greek for weekly options`, `Rho measures volatility`], answer: 1, explain: `Rho grows with time to expiration. Calls have positive rho, puts negative.` },
          { q: `Every long option position has...`, options: [`Positive theta and negative gamma`, `Negative delta`, `Positive gamma, negative theta and positive vega`, `Zero rho`], answer: 2, explain: `Option buyers pay time decay in exchange for gamma and vega exposure.` },
          { q: `A call has delta 0.50, theta -0.05 and vega 0.10. Overnight the stock rises $1 and IV falls 3 points. Ignoring gamma, the call changes by about...`, options: [`+$0.15 per share`, `+$0.50 per share`, `-$0.35 per share`, `+$0.25 per share`], answer: 0, explain: `+0.50 from delta, -0.05 from theta, -0.30 from vega: net +$0.15 per share.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: `A $100 stock has options with an implied volatility of 40%. Using the rule of 16, the typical one-day move priced in is about...`, options: [`$0.40`, `$16`, `$2.50`, `$4`], answer: 2, explain: `40 / 16 = 2.5%, and 2.5% of $100 is $2.50.` },
    { q: `Leo owns 2 calls with delta 0.55 and is short 1 put with delta -0.30. What is his total position delta, in share equivalents?`, options: [`+140 shares`, `+80 shares`, `+25 shares`, `-140 shares`], answer: 0, explain: `Calls: 2 x 0.55 x 100 = +110. The short put flips -0.30 to +0.30, adding +30. Total +140.` },
    { q: `Ms. Ortiz owns a call with theta -0.05 and vega 0.10. Overnight the stock is unchanged and IV falls 4 points. About how much does her contract change?`, options: [`-$5`, `+$35`, `-$40`, `-$45`], answer: 3, explain: `Theta costs $0.05 and vega costs 0.10 x 4 = $0.40. Total -$0.45 per share, or -$45 per contract.` },
    { q: `Which change would raise the price of a put option, all else equal?`, options: [`The stock price rises`, `Implied volatility rises`, `Interest rates rise`, `An expected dividend is cancelled`], answer: 1, explain: `Higher IV raises all option prices. A rising stock, rising rates and a cancelled dividend all push put prices down.` },
    { q: `Before earnings, a $100 stock's at-the-money call plus put cost $8 in total. After the report the stock moves $5 and IV collapses. What is the most likely result for someone who bought both options?`, options: [`A loss, because the move was smaller than the roughly $8 move priced in`, `A big profit, because the stock moved`, `Exactly break even`, `A profit, because IV crush helps buyers`], answer: 0, explain: `The combined price implied roughly an $8 move. A $5 move leaves the pair worth about $5 plus little time value, below the $8 paid.` },
  ],
};
